           content. In this Primer document, the wrapper and set
            elements are referred to as <strong>structural
            elements</strong>, whereas the elements actually holding
            data are called <strong>content elements</strong>. Since a
            complete LIDO record can have a dozen or more nesting
            levels, some distinctions between kinds of elements are made
            in the LIDO element descriptions.</p>
            <p>The following figure shows the nesting of LIDO structural
            and content elements with a corresponding example for the
            description of materials and techniques, used in the
            production of an object. The <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectMaterialsTechWrap">Object
            Materials/Techniques Wrapper</a> contains the <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectMaterialsTechSet">Object
            Materials/Techniques Set</a>, enclosing the <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#displayMaterialsTech">Display
            Materials/Techniques</a> element and the set for <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#materialsTech">Materials/Techniques</a>,
            holding the index elements.</p>
            <figure>
            <img src="img/figure_nesting.png"
            alt="Figure 6: Nesting of structural and content elements" />
            <figcaption aria-hidden="true">Figure 6: Nesting of
            structural and content elements</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Following is an XML code
            snippet containing the elements depicted in figure 5.</p>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb10"><pre
            class="sourceCode xml"><code class="sourceCode xml"><span id="cb10-1"><a href="#cb10-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a> &lt;<span class="kw">lido:objectMaterialsTechWrap</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-2"><a href="#cb10-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;<span class="kw">lido:objectMaterialsTechSet</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-3"><a href="#cb10-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;<span class="kw">lido:displayMaterialsTech</span><span class="ot"> xml:lang=</span><span class="st">&quot;{...}&quot;</span>&gt;[...]&lt;/<span class="kw">lido:displayMaterialsTech</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-4"><a href="#cb10-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;<span class="kw">lido:materialsTech</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-5"><a href="#cb10-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>            &lt;<span class="kw">lido:termMaterialsTech</span><span class="ot"> lido:type=</span><span class="st">&quot;{...}&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-6"><a href="#cb10-6" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>                &lt;<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span><span class="ot"> rdf:about=</span><span class="st">&quot;{...}&quot;</span>&gt;&lt;/<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-7"><a href="#cb10-7" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>            &lt;/<span class="kw">lido:termMaterialsTech</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-8"><a href="#cb10-8" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;/<span class="kw">lido:materialsTech</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-9"><a href="#cb10-9" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;/<span class="kw">lido:objectMaterialsTechSet</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb10-10"><a href="#cb10-10" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;/<span class="kw">lido:objectMaterialsTechWrap</span>&gt;</span></code></pre></div>
            <h3 data-number="4.3.1" id="structural-elements"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.3.1</span> Structural
            elements</h3>
            <p>LIDO defines structural elements for grouping more
            specific elements together. A structural element is either a
            <strong>wrapper</strong> which is non-repeatable, or a
            repeatable <strong>set</strong>. Usually, wrappers are on a
            higher nesting level relative to set elements, the latter
            being typically enclosed by a wrapper. However, wrapper
            elements may also occur as sub-elements of wrappers as well
            as sets.</p>
            <h4 data-number="4.3.1.1" id="wrapper-elements"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.3.1.1</span> Wrapper
            elements</h4>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> In the <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html">LIDO
            Schema Documentation</a>, an element is introduced as “A
            wrapper …” if it structures the data in a LIDO record and if
            it is not repeatable.</p>
            <p><strong>Features:</strong> Wrappers are not repeatable,
            have no attributes, except for a language attribute that can
            be given to administrative and descriptive metadata. A
            wrapper itself does not hold any content data.</p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectMaterialsTechWrap">lido:classificationWrap</a>&gt;
            is a wrapper for the index element &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#classification">lido:classification</a>&gt;,
            and is contained by the wrapper element &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectClassificationWrap">lido:objectClassificationWrap</a>&gt;.</p>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb11"><pre
            class="sourceCode xml"><code class="sourceCode xml"><span id="cb11-1"><a href="#cb11-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;<span class="kw">lido:classificationWrap</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb11-2"><a href="#cb11-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;<span class="kw">lido:classification</span><span class="ot"> lido:type=</span><span class="st">&quot;{...}&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb11-3"><a href="#cb11-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span><span class="ot"> rdf:about=</span><span class="st">&quot;{...}&quot;</span>&gt;&lt;/<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb11-4"><a href="#cb11-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;/<span class="kw">lido:classification</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb11-5"><a href="#cb11-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;/<span class="kw">lido:classificationWrap</span>&gt;</span></code></pre></div>
            <h4 data-number="4.3.1.2" id="set-elements"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.3.1.2</span> Set
            elements</h4>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> In the <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html">LIDO
            Schema documentation</a>, an element is introduced as “A set
            …” if it structures the data in a LIDO record and if it is
            repeatable.</p>
            <p><strong>Features:</strong> In contrast to LIDO wrappers,
            sets are repeatable and can have attributes. Like wrappers,
            they do not hold any content data.</p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#measurementsSet">lido:measurementsSet</a>&gt;
            is a set element that encloses three index elements for
            object dimensions (&lt;lido:measurementType&gt;,
            &lt;lido:measurementUnit&gt;,
            &lt;lido:measurementValue&gt;), all of which are mandatory
            if &lt;lido:measurementsSet&gt; is used. The set is
            repeatable within its parent element so that it can
            accommodate data for different types of dimensions, such as
            height, width, weight, etc. The following XML code snippet
            shows the usage of measurement types “height” and
            “width”.</p>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb12"><pre
            class="sourceCode